###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# Training took about ten days on four A100 GPUs; the DFT data about six
# million CPU hours. MD speed (Table I): 2.35e7 atom-steps per second on one
# A100 for 14 million Cu atoms, about 1.5e8 atom-steps per second on eight
# A100s for 100 million atoms; the timing on Cu is held in the encoding
# input.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - NEP4 is introduced here as a version of NEP (registry method NEP, origin fan2021); no separate method is recorded.
# - The formation-energy MAEs of EAM (695, 1122 meV/atom) are not encoded (EAM is not an MLIP); MACE-MP-0 (medium) values are only in Fig. 2d, e.
# - Property MAEs (elastic constants, surface and vacancy energies, melting points, phonons; Fig. 3e) are not accuracy metrics of the schema.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: crossref).
# - Evaluation roles and data, base models, inference timings with their system sizes, and the source location of each value are recorded in the encoding input (artifacts/kg/encoding/specs/song2024.yaml) until the schema has terms for them.

